#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5535b1d3-0529-5411-b513-6a8eeebc8b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330969} {:answer/id #uuid "2e60d34e-02b2-5978-b117-a667cb9ca579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330970} {:answer/id #uuid "51b22bb7-db06-5bad-9384-34af6ed679e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330971} {:answer/id #uuid "62be1fc5-e9da-52c3-914b-0cf766b7145d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330972} {:answer/id #uuid "c75587ad-2a40-5b14-91f0-e672131fad70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=4x+3\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330973}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62be1fc5-e9da-52c3-914b-0cf766b7145d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330972} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae4e4c26-14dd-5edd-9ebd-badb52824707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65b6a4c9-d497-51fc-b78a-818d9d90b8fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132587" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=2xy+2$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)(2)+2 \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(1,2)}(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =6(x-1) \\\\ z & =6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ z=6x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f43633ae-9118-5fb5-8924-8d9d38ed6b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} {:answer/id #uuid "dd66e5b1-b0d7-510d-902f-6af28619ad8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330977} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3342aa-db4c-58cc-810a-00e95c556260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330978} {:answer/id #uuid "9ad33dab-823c-5cbf-aae5-94ac4bbd9ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330979} {:answer/id #uuid "f5e4bec9-e9a6-5d37-acc4-6b6af9581f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f43633ae-9118-5fb5-8924-8d9d38ed6b6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330976} :answer-field/id #uuid "42ac90ee-1bdb-505d-b1b9-a5d78e62d51e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90feb6cc-3983-5462-9881-e7dbb7d7a0f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-132595" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Given that the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=-\\frac{2{x}^{2}+y}{{y}^{3}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfd92310-90b2-582f-84db-234cd0ca623c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330983} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ce60a-93d2-53e6-8882-c89d623a9e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330984} {:answer/id #uuid "770e6717-4d6d-56b4-b15d-1143ab390c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330985} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd669da-ce2e-57b5-af70-442799426116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330986} {:answer/id #uuid "1e2ced03-0d0e-5009-83cc-26a21ff53991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j+3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca3ce60a-93d2-53e6-8882-c89d623a9e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330984} :answer-field/id #uuid "38009d16-d2ad-5654-ae55-e75bed727df8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e93bbd2-d64e-547a-8b0e-91b9a4e6aa38" :question/math-academy-id "q-132601" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,3)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,2,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0325887-ce25-5d6f-bf27-d85541e1dbe0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Vector to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead71636-829d-5b75-b1c0-ecd981622383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330990} {:answer/id #uuid "9dea61ef-3d3f-5c8a-a8d2-ed073c9afab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330991} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e5ac1-ca46-526f-afed-e1507f7a295c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-1,0,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330992} {:answer/id #uuid "58f6f420-e3f7-5eda-ac45-92e3f2d2934c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330993} {:answer/id #uuid "1d94c907-2e6e-55f1-8424-a786cc1724e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f6f420-e3f7-5eda-ac45-92e3f2d2934c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330993} :answer-field/id #uuid "546c7e1b-42aa-5583-a579-3cc21ab4220e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e7a0ef21-1ab3-5b2b-a1e5-8efa25c0c00b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132602" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=1.$ Given that the point $P(0,1,7)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124874 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5685f7-71b2-58ae-8c61-f555b355d92f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ddb6af-120d-52d3-be31-592935d74472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330996} {:answer/id #uuid "f850724e-25f5-5e89-8e68-e4abe88f8e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330997} {:answer/id #uuid "e684320d-ce87-5f60-bb04-d724d6efad35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=-\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330998} {:answer/id #uuid "7255656e-761f-5d0d-a32f-6d8c36a489a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330999} {:answer/id #uuid "8acdb7d9-89f8-54b4-b208-45a3778ef589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{4}{5}y-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7255656e-761f-5d0d-a32f-6d8c36a489a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330999} :answer-field/id #uuid "896bf1f0-ba4b-535d-8282-a74335b3f33e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "491a90b9-f33c-5937-8491-8e8447d7cf82" :question/math-academy-id "q-36219" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,4)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{3x+y}{\\sqrt{6xy+{y}^{2}}}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(1,2,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,2) & =\\frac{3(1)+(2)}{\\sqrt{6(1)(2)+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(1,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(1,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{5}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,2,4)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{4}y+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
